## koot: Adaptive Security Organism & Capability Matrix

[cite_start]koot is a platform-agnostic, multi-backend secret subsystem designed for high-stakes environments[cite: 671]. [cite_start]It operates on the principle of "Information Agnosticism," treating all data as a raw binary payload, but is fully aware of the hardware it runs on and its own operational health[cite: 672]. 

### 1. The Adaptive Registry Bus (The Central Nervous System)
[cite_start]The foundation of the system is a completely hollow registry bus that manages swappable plugins[cite: 673]. [cite_start]It does not hardcode operations; it provides sockets[cite: 674].
* [cite_start]**Environment Sensor Plugin:** This module actively probes the host system upon boot[cite: 675].
* [cite_start]**Hardware-Driven Loading:** If it detects hardware acceleration like AES-NI or a TPM 2.0 chip, it automatically mounts high-performance cryptography and identity drivers[cite: 676]. [cite_start]If it detects a low-power device, it defaults to memory-efficient fallback drivers[cite: 677].
* [cite_start]**Dynamic Throttling:** The system monitors real-time CPU thermal loads and available RAM[cite: 678]. [cite_start]If a massive file (e.g., a 10GB video) is being processed and RAM dips to critical levels, the system automatically shrinks its "Chunking Engine" segment size to prevent crashing the host[cite: 679].

### 2. The Dynamic Schema & Capability Matrix (The Opt-In Taxonomy)
[cite_start]koot acts as a highly customizable, a la carte menu for data protection, driven by a Dynamic Schema & Capability Matrix[cite: 659, 669]. 
* [cite_start]**On-Demand Capability Directory:** The system will have a queryable manifest[cite: 660]. [cite_start]Upon explicit request, it will output a comprehensive list of every data type it currently knows how to handle (e.g., standard credentials, 4K media, biometric hashes, genomic sequences, health records)[cite: 661].
* [cite_start]**Opt-In Activation:** The user is in total control of the vault's scope[cite: 662]. [cite_start]You can choose to activate only specific data profiles[cite: 663]. [cite_start]For example, if you only want it to handle documents and passwords, you can leave the "health records" and "biometrics" modules deactivated, and the system will actively reject those data types to minimize its attack surface[cite: 664].
* [cite_start]**Modular Schema "Hot-Swapping":** The definitions for these data types will act as plug-and-play modules[cite: 665]. [cite_start]In the future, if a new type of sensitive data emerges (or if you simply want to add a "Medical Pack" of schemas), you can plug that specific group of schemas into the system[cite: 666]. [cite_start]Conversely, you can completely unplug a group of schemas, entirely removing the system's ability to process them[cite: 667].

### 3. Analytics & Telemetry Engine (The Observer)
[cite_start]A dedicated, read-only analytics plugin sits passively on the bus, observing the system's state without ever having access to the plaintext data[cite: 680].
* [cite_start]**Operational Analytics:** It tracks core efficiency metrics, including encryption throughput, API response times, and storage adapter latency[cite: 681].
* [cite_start]**Security Posture Monitoring:** It actively logs failed authentication attempts, connection drops at the mTLS gateway, and any chunk-corruption rates in the storage backend[cite: 682].
* [cite_start]**Real-Time State Output:** It generates a continuous, encrypted JSON telemetry stream that can be ingested by a larger host system's dashboard to display the vault's health in real-time[cite: 683].

### 4. Governance, Audit & Emergency Matrix (The Conscience & Override)
[cite_start]This is the critical accountability and control layer ensuring the automated system never goes rogue[cite: 684].
* [cite_start]**Immutable Audit Log:** Every single access attempt, modification, or migration event is recorded in a tamper-proof, append-only signed log[cite: 685]. [cite_start]This provides a permanent, cryptographic record of who accessed what and when[cite: 686].
* [cite_start]**The "Dead Man's Switch" & Nuke Protocol:** A modular trigger that executes automated emergency actions—like vault wiping, locking, or ownership transfer—during a detected breach or critical security violation[cite: 687].
* [cite_start]**The Override Matrix (Human-in-the-Loop):** A strict manual override protocol supersedes automation[cite: 688]. [cite_start]Through a Master Interlock (triggered via a unique hardware key or master passphrase), an admin can instantly pause, reverse, or force any operation[cite: 689]. [cite_start]Furthermore, Critical Action Halts ensure that extreme automated actions (like triggering the Dead Man's switch or migrating massive databases due to disk failure) pause and request manual authorization before proceeding[cite: 690].

### 5. Agnostic Envelope & Entropy Pipeline (DNA & Identity)
* [cite_start]**The Secret Envelope:** Every piece of data—whether a standard password, a 4K video, or a bio-template—is wrapped in a universal container[cite: 691]. [cite_start]This envelope features a specific Header (containing the version, content-type, crypto-suite-ID, and IV/nonce) and a Merkle-Tree signed Payload[cite: 692].
* [cite_start]**Multi-Stage Auth Pipeline:** The system utilizes an "Entropy Chain" rather than a simple login[cite: 693]. [cite_start]It combines multiple entropy sources (passwords, keyfiles, biometric hashes) through the memory-hard Argon2id algorithm to derive a highly secure, volatile Master Key that is instantly wiped from RAM after use[cite: 694].

### 6. Hybrid-PQC & Adaptive Storage (The Armor & Muscle)
* [cite_start]**Quantum-Safe Cryptography:** The security driver uses a dual-layer Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) strategy[cite: 695]. [cite_start]It combines classical AES-256-GCM with lattice-based ML-KEM (Kyber), ensuring the data is mathematically protected against both current supercomputers and future quantum threats[cite: 696].
* [cite_start]**Multi-Backend Migration & Mirroring:** Storage is handled by swappable adapters for Local Filesystems, SQL databases (like SQLite or PostgreSQL), and Cloud Object Storage (S3)[cite: 697]. [cite_start]The system can natively execute Live Migrations, mirroring or fully moving data across entirely different backends without ever decrypting the payloads[cite: 698].
* **Self-Healing Storage:** The system actively looks for bit-rot. [cite_start]If a chunk of data is corrupted in one database, the system automatically pulls and restores the clean chunk from a mirrored storage backend[cite: 699].

### 7. Zero-Trust Connectivity (The Gates)
[cite_start]To operate as a networked subsystem, koot treats all connections as inherently hostile[cite: 700].
* [cite_start]**Mutual TLS (mTLS):** For remote or network-based requests, the API sits behind an mTLS gateway[cite: 701]. It strictly requires and validates client-side X.509 certificates. [cite_start]If an unauthorized device pings the server, the connection drops before a password is even requested[cite: 702].
* [cite_start]**Local IPC & Machine Identity:** When acting as a localized subsystem for a host application, it communicates via local Unix Sockets or Named Pipes[cite: 703]. [cite_start]It can also utilize "AppRole" Machine Identity, reading the host's hardware TPM 2.0 signature to unlock the vault for the larger system without human intervention[cite: 704].